-- For domains, as OCaml 5's: computations run truly at once,
-- each on a thread of the system of its own, and what synchronises
-- them -- cells every domain reads and writes (`Atomic`), mutexes and
-- conditions.  The base a scheduler is written on, in heff: its workers
-- are domains.  Import it by a name, `import std.domain as Domain`.
--
-- > [!CAUTION]
-- > Unstable: concurrency in heff is not settled. What this module offers
-- > may change, or go, in any release.
--
-- ```haskell
-- import co
-- import std.domain as Domain
-- import std.io
--
-- -- | `n` added to the counter, one at a time
-- adding : {e : HFunctor}
--        -> {{dm : Domain.domain <: e}}
--        -> Domain.Atomic Int
--        -> Int
--        -> Co e Unit
-- adding counter 0 = pure ()
-- adding counter n = do
--   _ <- Domain.fetchAdd counter 1
--   adding counter (n - 1)
--
-- counted : {e : HFunctor}
--         -> {{io : stdio <: e}}
--         -> {{dm : Domain.domain <: e}}
--         -> Co e Unit
-- counted = do
--   counter <- Domain.newAtomic 0
--   with s := Domain.scope do
--     _ <- Domain.spawn s (adding counter 1000)
--     _ <- Domain.spawn s (adding counter 1000)
--     pure ()
--   n <- Domain.get counter
--   print (debug n)
-- ```
--
-- Operation | What it does
-- --- | ---
-- `with s := scope do ss` | `ss` run with `s` a scope: the `with` waits, at its end, for every domain spawned in it
-- `spawn s c` | `c` run on a domain of its own, one of the scope's; this one goes on
-- `cancel s` | the scope's mark set: no domain stopped
-- `cancelled s` | whether the mark is set: by `cancel`, a domain of the scope failing, or its body
-- `join d` | the domain's answer, waiting for it to end
-- `tried c` | `c` run here: `Right` its answer, or `Left why` where it fails
-- `fail why` | this domain fails as `why` says: a `tried`'s `Left` handed on
-- `cores` | how many domains the machine runs at once
-- `newAtomic v` | a cell holding `v`, for every domain
-- `get a` / `set a v` | what it holds; ... and it holds `v` now
-- `cas a seen v` | it holds `v` now, where it held `seen`: whether it did
-- `fetchAdd a n` | `n` added to the number it holds: what it held
-- `newMutex` / `lock m` / `unlock m` | a mutex, held by one domain at a time
-- `newCondition` | a condition, for domains to wait on
-- `wait c m` | `m` let go of, until the condition is signalled, then held again
-- `signal c` / `broadcast c` | one domain waiting on `c` woken; ... every one
-- `newQueue` | a queue, for every domain: what is put in it is taken out once
-- `put q v` / `take q` | `v` put at the back, never waiting; the front taken out, waiting while empty
-- `forever v` | `v`, and all it holds, kept for as long as the program runs: counted by no domain
-- `rooted c` | `c`'s custodian the runtime's custodians, for a world that names none: its `with`s in their custody, a failure at the root the program's
--
-- `cas` compares by identity, as OCaml's `Atomic.compare_and_set`: a
-- number of a word or a constructor without fields is itself, anything
-- else the value that was read, not an equal one built again.  A `wait` may end
-- without a signal: what it waits for is checked again, in a loop.  A
-- domain's failure is its joiner's, as it failed -- unless the join is
-- in a `tried`, which gives the failure back as a `Left` instead: so a
-- failure of this domain's own (an `abandon`, a runtime's), or of one
-- it joins, is caught where it is to be; `fail` hands a failure caught
-- on, its words kept, as a join does.  What is put in a queue
-- is handed to the one domain that takes it, first in first out: a
-- `take` waits while the queue is empty, a `put` never waits.
--
-- Domains are spawned in a scope, and only there: structured
-- concurrency.  `with s := Domain.scope do ss` runs `ss` here, in the
-- block's world with `scoped`, the scope's own, and, at its end, waits
-- for every domain spawned in the scope, the body free to do other work
-- meanwhile.  `spawn s c` is `scoped`'s: only the body spawns in the
-- scope.  `c` is a computation of the world around the `with`, which
-- has no `scoped`, so a domain cannot spawn in its own scope -- one that
-- wants domains of its own opens a scope of its own.  A domain of the
-- scope that fails fails the scope: the `with` fails, once all of them
-- have ended, as the first to fail did; one where the body fails, as
-- the body did.
--
-- Cancelling is cooperative: the scope carries a mark, set by `cancel`,
-- or where one of its domains fails, or its body does, and each domain
-- looks at it where it chooses (`cancelled`) and winds up as it
-- chooses.  No domain is stopped from outside: stopped at an arbitrary
-- operation, it would keep a mutex it holds locked, and the release of
-- what it holds would not run.  A domain in a call of the system that
-- waits, or in a loop with no operation, ends only where it looks at
-- the mark; a loop that never ends is fuel's to stop.  A body left by a
-- throw handled outside the `with` (an `exception`'s) leaves its
-- domains running, not waited for.
--
-- A domain's computation is of the world around its scope's `with`, and
-- every handler between there and the program's world runs it too, on
-- its domain (a domain of the runtime's can only be given the program's
-- world's computations, and those it is given are the spawn's, handled
-- on their way to it); `tried`'s runs here, at the call, in what is
-- around it.  So the schedulers written on domains -- cml's, boc's --
-- take their threads and behaviours as closed computations of their own
-- world instead, and each runs them on its workers, in a scope of its
-- own, with none of the handlers around where they were spawned.
--
-- What runs the domains is the backend's runtime, the program the same
-- on each.  A domain, a cell, a mutex and a condition are the
-- runtime's own, held by the program as values of no constructor, and
-- each is freed with the program's last reference to it -- a cell
-- letting go of what it holds, a domain of its answer.  The C
-- backend's (domain.provider.c): a domain is a thread of the system, a
-- cell is changed atomically, a mutex and a condition are the system's.
-- What a domain gives back is shared (`heff_share`), not copied; what a
-- domain is given, and what is put in a queue, is moved where the
-- domain giving it held it alone (`heff_move`: counted by the taker
-- alone from then on), and shared otherwise.  A domain whose last reference goes while
-- it still runs is freed as its thread ends.  The program ends where
-- `main` does, domains not joined with it.
--
-- JavaScript's (domain.provider.js): one domain, as one core runs
-- threads.  The domains take turns, each at its operations of this
-- effect: at each, the others ready go first, so a domain waiting for
-- a cell another changes lets it be changed.  One waiting on a mutex,
-- a condition or a domain's end waits there, the others going on.
-- `cores` is 1.  A program whose domains all wait, for one another,
-- stops there, said by nothing: the page cannot tell it from one
-- waiting for something of the world's.

module std.domain
  ( domain
  , Domain
  , Atomic
  , Mutex
  , Condition
  , Queue
  , Scope
  , Scoping
  , scoped
  , scope
  , spawn
  , cancel
  , cancelled
  , join
  , tried
  , fail
  , cores
  , newAtomic
  , get
  , set
  , cas
  , fetchAdd
  , newMutex
  , lock
  , unlock
  , newCondition
  , wait
  , signal
  , broadcast
  , newQueue
  , put
  , take
  , forever
  , instanceBinderScope
  , instanceHolderScope
  , rooted
  )

import co
import custodian as Custodian

-- | A domain whose answer is an `a`.  (The runtime's own, a foreign
-- | value: no value of the program has it.)
data Domain a

-- | A cell holding an `a`, for every domain.  (The runtime's own, a
-- | foreign value: no value of the program has it.)
data Atomic a

-- | A mutex.  (The runtime's own, a foreign value: no value of the
-- | program has it.)
data Mutex

-- | A condition.  (The runtime's own, a foreign value: no value of the
-- | program has it.)
data Condition

-- | A queue of `a`s, for every domain: what one domain puts in, exactly
-- | one domain takes out, first in first out.  (The runtime's own, a
-- | foreign value: no value of the program has it.)
data Queue a

-- | A value of some type, the type hidden: what an operation of a type
-- | of its own holds (the effect has no parameter for it).
type Some (f : Type -> Type) = (r : Type) -> ((a : Type) -> f a -> r) -> r

-- (what it holds, changed, of whatever type)
someThen : {f g : Type -> Type}
         -> ({a : Type} -> f a -> g a)
         -> Some f
         -> Some g
someThen h s = \r use -> s r (\a v -> use a (h v))

data Spawning (m : Type -> Type) x a = MkSpawning (m a) (Domain a -> x)

data Joining x a = MkJoining (Domain a) (a -> x)

-- (the computation is given as a function of `Unit`, computed only where
-- it is tried: a handler between, a scheduler's, maps it without
-- computing it -- else what it computes before its first operation would
-- be computed there, outside the `tried`)
data Trying (m : Type -> Type) x a
  = MkTrying (Unit -> m a) (Either String a -> x)

data Making x a = MkMaking a (Atomic a -> x)

data Reading x a = MkReading (Atomic a) (a -> x)

data Writing x a = MkWriting (Atomic a) a x

data Comparing x a = MkComparing (Atomic a) a a (Bool -> x)

data MakingQueue x a = MkMakingQueue (Queue a -> x)

data Putting x a = MkPutting (Queue a) a x

data Taking x a = MkTaking (Queue a) (a -> x)

data Keeping x a = MkKeeping a (a -> x)

spawningThen : {m : Type -> Type}
             -> {x y a : Type}
             -> (x -> y)
             -> Spawning m x a
             -> Spawning m y a
spawningThen g (MkSpawning c k) = MkSpawning c (\d -> g (k d))

spawningBy : {m n : Type -> Type}
           -> {x a : Type}
           -> ({t : Type} -> m t -> n t)
           -> Spawning m x a
           -> Spawning n x a
spawningBy nt (MkSpawning c k) = MkSpawning (nt c) k

joiningThen : {x y a : Type} -> (x -> y) -> Joining x a -> Joining y a
joiningThen g (MkJoining d k) = MkJoining d (\v -> g (k v))

tryingThen : {m : Type -> Type}
           -> {x y a : Type}
           -> (x -> y)
           -> Trying m x a
           -> Trying m y a
tryingThen g (MkTrying c k) = MkTrying c (\r -> g (k r))

tryingBy : {m n : Type -> Type}
         -> {x a : Type}
         -> ({t : Type} -> m t -> n t)
         -> Trying m x a
         -> Trying n x a
tryingBy nt (MkTrying c k) = MkTrying (\u -> nt (c u)) k

makingThen : {x y a : Type} -> (x -> y) -> Making x a -> Making y a
makingThen g (MkMaking v k) = MkMaking v (\at -> g (k at))

readingThen : {x y a : Type} -> (x -> y) -> Reading x a -> Reading y a
readingThen g (MkReading at k) = MkReading at (\v -> g (k v))

writingThen : {x y a : Type} -> (x -> y) -> Writing x a -> Writing y a
writingThen g (MkWriting at v x) = MkWriting at v (g x)

comparingThen : {x y a : Type} -> (x -> y) -> Comparing x a -> Comparing y a
comparingThen g (MkComparing at seen v k) =
  MkComparing at seen v (\b -> g (k b))

makingQueueThen : {x y a : Type}
                -> (x -> y)
                -> MakingQueue x a
                -> MakingQueue y a
makingQueueThen g (MkMakingQueue k) = MkMakingQueue (\q -> g (k q))

puttingThen : {x y a : Type} -> (x -> y) -> Putting x a -> Putting y a
puttingThen g (MkPutting q v x) = MkPutting q v (g x)

takingThen : {x y a : Type} -> (x -> y) -> Taking x a -> Taking y a
takingThen g (MkTaking q k) = MkTaking q (\v -> g (k v))

keepingThen : {x y a : Type} -> (x -> y) -> Keeping x a -> Keeping y a
keepingThen g (MkKeeping v k) = MkKeeping v (\u -> g (k u))

-- (an operation holding a computation: a domain spawned, a computation
-- tried, and the custodian's, from `rooted`)
data HoldingF (m : Type -> Type) x
  = Spawn (Some (Spawning m x))
  | Tried (Some (Trying m x))
  | CustodyOp (Maybe Custodian.Custodian) (Custodian.CustodianF m x)

-- (an operation holding none: the same whatever its computations are)
data PlainF x
  = Join (Some (Joining x))
  | Fail String
  | Cores (Int -> x)
  | NewAtomic (Some (Making x))
  | GetAtomic (Some (Reading x))
  | SetAtomic (Some (Writing x))
  | CasAtomic (Some (Comparing x))
  | FetchAdd (Atomic Int) Int (Int -> x)
  | NewMutex (Mutex -> x)
  | Lock Mutex x
  | Unlock Mutex x
  | NewCondition (Condition -> x)
  | Wait Condition Mutex x
  | Signal Condition x
  | Broadcast Condition x
  | NewQueue (Some (MakingQueue x))
  | PutQueue (Some (Putting x))
  | TakeQueue (Some (Taking x))
  | Forever (Some (Keeping x))

-- (the operations, those holding computations apart: what a handler
-- between maps is those few, and every other operation passes it as it
-- is, at no cost but a look at its tag)
data DomainF (m : Type -> Type) x = Holding (HoldingF m x) | Plain (PlainF x)

holdingThen : {m : Type -> Type}
            -> {x y : Type}
            -> (x -> y)
            -> HoldingF m x
            -> HoldingF m y
holdingThen g (Spawn s) = Spawn (someThen (spawningThen g) s)
holdingThen g (Tried s) = Tried (someThen (tryingThen g) s)
holdingThen g (CustodyOp at q) = CustodyOp at (Custodian.custodianThen g q)

holdingBy : {m n : Type -> Type}
          -> {x : Type}
          -> ({t : Type} -> m t -> n t)
          -> HoldingF m x
          -> HoldingF n x
holdingBy nt (Spawn s) = Spawn (someThen (spawningBy nt) s)
holdingBy nt (Tried s) = Tried (someThen (tryingBy nt) s)
holdingBy nt (CustodyOp at q) = CustodyOp at (Custodian.custodianBy nt q)

plainThen : {x y : Type} -> (x -> y) -> PlainF x -> PlainF y
plainThen g (Join s) = Join (someThen (joiningThen g) s)
plainThen g (Fail why) = Fail why
plainThen g (Cores k) = Cores (\n -> g (k n))
plainThen g (NewAtomic s) = NewAtomic (someThen (makingThen g) s)
plainThen g (GetAtomic s) = GetAtomic (someThen (readingThen g) s)
plainThen g (SetAtomic s) = SetAtomic (someThen (writingThen g) s)
plainThen g (CasAtomic s) = CasAtomic (someThen (comparingThen g) s)
plainThen g (FetchAdd at n k) = FetchAdd at n (\o -> g (k o))
plainThen g (NewMutex k) = NewMutex (\mu -> g (k mu))
plainThen g (Lock mu x) = Lock mu (g x)
plainThen g (Unlock mu x) = Unlock mu (g x)
plainThen g (NewCondition k) = NewCondition (\cd -> g (k cd))
plainThen g (Wait cd mu x) = Wait cd mu (g x)
plainThen g (Signal cd x) = Signal cd (g x)
plainThen g (Broadcast cd x) = Broadcast cd (g x)
plainThen g (NewQueue s) = NewQueue (someThen (makingQueueThen g) s)
plainThen g (PutQueue s) = PutQueue (someThen (puttingThen g) s)
plainThen g (TakeQueue s) = TakeQueue (someThen (takingThen g) s)
plainThen g (Forever s) = Forever (someThen (keepingThen g) s)

-- | For OS threads, domains: spawned and joined, a failure caught, and
-- | what they share -- atomics, mutexes, conditions and queues.
domain : HFunctor
domain =
  { f = DomainF
  , hfmap = \m g p -> case p of
      Holding h -> Holding (holdingThen g h)
      Plain q -> Plain (plainThen g q)
  , hmap = \m n nt p -> case p of
      Holding h -> Holding (holdingBy nt h)
      Plain q -> Plain q
  }

-- Domains
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- (`c` run on a domain of its own, this one going on: a scope's, which
-- spawns in itself alone.  `c` is of this domain's world, `e`, so each
-- handler between here and the program's world runs it too, on its
-- domain)
started : {e : HFunctor}
        -> {a : Type}
        -> {{dm : domain <: e}}
        -> Co e a
        -> Co e (Domain a)
started c =
  op (dm (Holding (Spawn (\r use -> use a (MkSpawning c (\d -> d)))))) val

-- | The domain's answer, waiting for it to end; where it failed, this
-- | one fails as it did.
join : {e : HFunctor}
     -> {a : Type}
     -> {{dm : domain <: e}}
     -> Domain a
     -> Co e a
join d = op (dm (Plain (Join (\r use -> use a (MkJoining d (\v -> v)))))) val

-- | `c` run here, on this domain, its failure caught: `Right` its
-- | answer, or `Left why` where it fails -- abandons (`heff:abandon`),
-- | fails in the runtime, or joins a domain that failed -- the rest of
-- | `c` not run.
tried : {e : HFunctor}
      -> {a : Type}
      -> {{dm : domain <: e}}
      -> Co e a
      -> Co e (Either String a)
tried c =
  op (dm (Holding (Tried (\r use -> use a (MkTrying (\u -> c) (\r -> r)))))) val

-- | This domain fails as `why` says, as one joined fails where the
-- | domain it joins failed: caught by a `tried` around it, `Left why`,
-- | and else the domain's -- or, on main's, the program's, said.  So a
-- | failure caught (a `tried`'s `Left`) is handed on as it came, its
-- | words kept; `"heff:abandon"`, an abandon's, abandons.
fail : {e : HFunctor} -> {a : Type} -> {{dm : domain <: e}} -> String -> Co e a
fail why = op (dm (Plain (Fail why))) val

-- | How many domains the machine runs at once: its cores, as the system
-- | says (1 where it does not).
cores : {e : HFunctor} -> {{dm : domain <: e}} -> Co e Int
cores = op (dm (Plain (Cores (\n -> n)))) val

-- Cells
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- | A cell holding `v`, for every domain.
newAtomic : {e : HFunctor}
          -> {a : Type}
          -> {{dm : domain <: e}}
          -> a
          -> Co e (Atomic a)
newAtomic v =
  op (dm (Plain (NewAtomic (\r use -> use a (MkMaking v (\at -> at)))))) val

-- | What the cell holds.
get : {e : HFunctor} -> {a : Type} -> {{dm : domain <: e}} -> Atomic a -> Co e a
get at =
  op (dm (Plain (GetAtomic (\r use -> use a (MkReading at (\v -> v)))))) val

-- | ... and it holds `v` now.
set : {e : HFunctor}
    -> {a : Type}
    -> {{dm : domain <: e}}
    -> Atomic a
    -> a
    -> Co e Unit
set at v =
  op (dm (Plain (SetAtomic (\r use -> use a (MkWriting at v MkUnit))))) val

-- | The cell holds `v` now, where it held `seen` (compared by identity):
-- | whether it did.
cas : {e : HFunctor}
    -> {a : Type}
    -> {{dm : domain <: e}}
    -> Atomic a
    -> a
    -> a
    -> Co e Bool
cas at seen v =
  op
    (dm (Plain (CasAtomic (\r use -> use a (MkComparing at seen v (\b -> b))))))
    val

-- | `n` added to the number the cell holds: what it held.
fetchAdd : {e : HFunctor}
         -> {{dm : domain <: e}}
         -> Atomic Int
         -> Int
         -> Co e Int
fetchAdd at n = op (dm (Plain (FetchAdd at n (\o -> o)))) val

-- Mutexes and conditions
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- | A mutex, held by no domain.
newMutex : {e : HFunctor} -> {{dm : domain <: e}} -> Co e Mutex
newMutex = op (dm (Plain (NewMutex (\mu -> mu)))) val

-- | The mutex held by this domain, waiting while another holds it.
lock : {e : HFunctor} -> {{dm : domain <: e}} -> Mutex -> Co e Unit
lock mu = op (dm (Plain (Lock mu MkUnit))) val

-- | ... let go of: it fails where this domain does not hold it.
unlock : {e : HFunctor} -> {{dm : domain <: e}} -> Mutex -> Co e Unit
unlock mu = op (dm (Plain (Unlock mu MkUnit))) val

-- | A condition, for domains to wait on.
newCondition : {e : HFunctor} -> {{dm : domain <: e}} -> Co e Condition
newCondition = op (dm (Plain (NewCondition (\cd -> cd)))) val

-- | The mutex, held by this domain, let go of until the condition is
-- | signalled, and held again before it goes on.  It may end without a
-- | signal: what it waits for is checked again.
wait : {e : HFunctor} -> {{dm : domain <: e}} -> Condition -> Mutex -> Co e Unit
wait cd mu = op (dm (Plain (Wait cd mu MkUnit))) val

-- | One domain waiting on the condition woken, if one is.
signal : {e : HFunctor} -> {{dm : domain <: e}} -> Condition -> Co e Unit
signal cd = op (dm (Plain (Signal cd MkUnit))) val

-- | Every domain waiting on the condition woken.
broadcast : {e : HFunctor} -> {{dm : domain <: e}} -> Condition -> Co e Unit
broadcast cd = op (dm (Plain (Broadcast cd MkUnit))) val

-- Queues
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- | A queue, for every domain, holding nothing yet.
newQueue : {e : HFunctor}
         -> {a : Type}
         -> {{dm : domain <: e}}
         -> Co e (Queue a)
newQueue =
  op (dm (Plain (NewQueue (\r use -> use a (MkMakingQueue (\q -> q)))))) val

-- | `v` put at the back of the queue, for one domain to take; never
-- | waits.
put : {e : HFunctor}
    -> {a : Type}
    -> {{dm : domain <: e}}
    -> Queue a
    -> a
    -> Co e Unit
put q v =
  op (dm (Plain (PutQueue (\r use -> use a (MkPutting q v MkUnit))))) val

-- | The front of the queue taken out, this domain's alone now; waits
-- | while the queue is empty.
take : {e : HFunctor} -> {a : Type} -> {{dm : domain <: e}} -> Queue a -> Co e a
take q =
  op (dm (Plain (TakeQueue (\r use -> use a (MkTaking q (\v -> v)))))) val

-- Kept forever
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- | `v` itself, kept for as long as the program runs, with all it holds:
-- | counted by no domain from now on, as a definition's value is, and
-- | never let go of -- for what every domain reads all through a run,
-- | made once (a scheduler's cells, say), whose count would otherwise
-- | be changed by each domain at once.  What it holds is kept too: a
-- | cell's or a queue's contents are not, being changed.
forever : {e : HFunctor} -> {a : Type} -> {{dm : domain <: e}} -> a -> Co e a
forever v =
  op (dm (Plain (Forever (\r use -> use a (MkKeeping v (\u -> u)))))) val

-- Scopes
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- | A scope of domains, `with s := Domain.scope do`: its domains and
-- | its cancel mark.  `s` is the region of the `with` that made it, so
-- | the scope cannot leave the block; `w` is the world of the block
-- | around, its domains' world.
data Scope s (w : HFunctor)
  = MkScope (Atomic Bool) (Atomic (Maybe String)) (Atomic (List (Some Domain)))

-- (whether it is cancelled)
markOf : {s : Type} -> {w : HFunctor} -> Scope s w -> Atomic Bool
markOf (MkScope mark failure children) = mark

-- (why its first domain to fail failed)
failureOf : {s : Type} -> {w : HFunctor} -> Scope s w -> Atomic (Maybe String)
failureOf (MkScope mark failure children) = failure

-- (its domains)
childrenOf : {s : Type}
           -> {w : HFunctor}
           -> Scope s w
           -> Atomic (List (Some Domain))
childrenOf (MkScope mark failure children) = children

data SpawningIn (w : HFunctor) x a = MkSpawningIn (Co w a) (Domain a -> x)

spawningInThen : {w : HFunctor}
               -> {x y a : Type}
               -> (x -> y)
               -> SpawningIn w x a
               -> SpawningIn w y a
spawningInThen g (MkSpawningIn c k) = MkSpawningIn c (\d -> g (k d))

data ScopedF (w : HFunctor) (m : Type -> Type) x
  = SpawnIn (Some (SpawningIn w x))

-- | A scope's body's own: domains spawned in the scope, computations of
-- | `w`, the world of the block around the `with`.  Only the body has
-- | it, not a domain's computation: no domain spawns in its own scope.
scoped : HFunctor -> HFunctor
scoped w =
  { f = ScopedF w
  , hfmap = \m g p -> case p of
      SpawnIn s -> SpawnIn (someThen (spawningInThen g) s)
  , hmap = \m n nt p -> case p of
      SpawnIn s -> SpawnIn s
  }

-- | What a `with s := Domain.scope do` opens: a scope of domains.
data Scoping = OpenScope

-- | `with s := Domain.scope do ss`: `ss` run here, in the block's world
-- | with `scoped`, its own, `s` a scope its domains are spawned in
-- | (`spawn s c`); the `with` waits, at its end, for every domain
-- | spawned in it, and comes to what `ss` came to.
scope : Scoping
scope = OpenScope

-- | `c` run on a domain of its own, one of the scope's; this one goes on.
-- | The scope's `with` waits for it at its end, and `join` gives its
-- | answer.  Where it fails, the scope is cancelled, and fails at its
-- | end as the first of its domains to fail did.  Only the scope's body
-- | spawns in it: `c` is of the world around the `with`, `w`, where
-- | there is no `scoped`; each handler between there and the program's
-- | world runs it too, on its domain.
spawn : {e w : HFunctor}
      -> {s a : Type}
      -> {{sp : scoped w <: e}}
      -> Scope s w
      -> Co w a
      -> Co e (Domain a)
spawn sc c = op (sp (SpawnIn (\r use -> use a (MkSpawningIn c (\d -> d))))) val

-- | The scope cancelled: its mark set.  No domain of it is stopped:
-- | each looks at the mark where it chooses (`cancelled`), and winds up
-- | as it chooses.
cancel : {e w : HFunctor}
       -> {s : Type}
       -> {{dm : domain <: e}}
       -> Scope s w
       -> Co e Unit
cancel sc = set (markOf sc) True

-- | Whether the scope is cancelled: by `cancel`, or as one of its
-- | domains failed, or its body did.
cancelled : {e w : HFunctor}
          -> {s : Type}
          -> {{dm : domain <: e}}
          -> Scope s w
          -> Co e Bool
cancelled sc = get (markOf sc)

-- (`v` pushed onto the list the cell holds, again until no other domain
-- came between: a domain of an inner scope may spawn in this one)
pushed : {e : HFunctor}
       -> {a : Type}
       -> {{dm : domain <: e}}
       -> Atomic (List a)
       -> a
       -> Co e Unit
pushed cell v = do
  l <- get cell
  ok <- cas cell l (v :: l)
  if ok then pure () else pushed cell v

-- (a domain of the scope ended: its answer, or, where it failed, the
-- scope's failure, if it is the first, and its mark set, and this
-- domain fails as it did)
childEnded : {e w : HFunctor}
           -> {s a : Type}
           -> {{dm : domain <: e}}
           -> Scope s w
           -> Either String a
           -> Co e a
childEnded sc (Right v) = pure v
childEnded sc (Left why) = do
  _ <- cas (failureOf sc) Nothing (Just why)
  set (markOf sc) True
  fail why

-- (`c` spawned, one of the scope's)
spawnedIn : {e : HFunctor}
          -> {s a : Type}
          -> {{dm : domain <: e}}
          -> Scope s e
          -> Co e a
          -> Co e (Domain a)
spawnedIn sc c = do
  d <- started (bind (tried c) (childEnded sc))
  pushed (childrenOf sc) (\r use -> use a d)
  pure d

-- (a `spawn` of the body's, answered in the handler around it)
spawnAnswered : {e : HFunctor}
              -> {s x b : Type}
              -> {{dm : domain <: e}}
              -> Scope s e
              -> (hd : Handler e)
              -> SpawningIn e x b
              -> hd.M x
spawnAnswered sc hd (MkSpawningIn c k) =
  hd.bind (handle hd (spawnedIn sc c)) (\d -> hd.ret (k d))

-- (what the body's `scoped` does: its domains spawned in the scope)
scopeAlgebra : {e : HFunctor}
             -> {s x : Type}
             -> {{dm : domain <: e}}
             -> Scope s e
             -> (hd : Handler e)
             -> ScopedF e hd.M x
             -> hd.M x
scopeAlgebra sc hd (SpawnIn sp) = sp (hd.M x) (\b p -> spawnAnswered sc hd p)

-- (the domain waited for, how it ended said by the scope already)
waitedFor : {e : HFunctor} -> {{dm : domain <: e}} -> Some Domain -> Co e Unit
waitedFor child =
  child (Co e Unit) (\a d -> bind (tried (join d)) (\r -> pure ()))

-- (each domain waited for)
allWaitedFor : {e : HFunctor}
             -> {{dm : domain <: e}}
             -> List (Some Domain)
             -> Co e Unit
allWaitedFor [] = pure ()
allWaitedFor (d :: ds) = do
  waitedFor d
  allWaitedFor ds

-- (the body's answer, where none of the scope's domains failed)
answered : {e : HFunctor}
         -> {a : Type}
         -> {{dm : domain <: e}}
         -> Maybe String
         -> a
         -> Co e a
answered Nothing v = pure v
answered (Just why) v = fail why

-- (the scope's end, the body's: every domain spawned in it waited for
-- -- none is spawned once the body has ended -- then the body's
-- failure, or the first domain's, or the body's answer)
scopeEnded : {e w : HFunctor}
           -> {s a : Type}
           -> {{dm : domain <: e}}
           -> Scope s w
           -> Either String a
           -> Co e a
scopeEnded sc (Left why) = do
  set (markOf sc) True
  ds <- get (childrenOf sc)
  allWaitedFor ds
  fail why
scopeEnded sc (Right v) = do
  ds <- get (childrenOf sc)
  allWaitedFor ds
  why <- get (failureOf sc)
  answered why v

-- (a scope made, the body run with it, its `scoped` handled here, and
-- its domains waited for)
heldScope : {e : HFunctor}
          -> {r : Type}
          -> {{dm : domain <: e}}
          -> (Scope Unit e -> Co (scoped e <+> e) (r, Scope Unit e))
          -> Co e r
heldScope body = do
  mark <- newAtomic False
  failure <- newAtomic Nothing
  children <- newAtomic Nil
  sc <- pure (MkScope mark failure children)
  ended <-
    tried
      (within
         (scoped e)
         e
         (scopeAlgebra sc)
         (bind (body sc) (\p -> pure (fst p))))
  scopeEnded sc ended

-- | A `with` of a scope: its name the scope, of the world of the block
-- | around, its body in that world with `scoped`, and the `with` comes
-- | to what the body came to.
instance instanceBinderScope : Binder Scoping
instanceBinderScope =
  { Contents = \w s -> Scope s w
  , World = \w -> scoped w <+> w
  , Result = \r -> r
  }

-- | A scope held by a `with`: made, the body run with it, and every
-- | domain spawned in it waited for at the body's end -- where the body
-- | fails too, the scope cancelled first.  Then the `with` fails as the
-- | body did, or as the first of the scope's domains to fail did, or
-- | comes to the body's answer.
instance instanceHolderScope : {e : HFunctor}
                             -> {{dm : domain <: e}}
                             -> Holder
                               e
                               Scoping
                               (\s -> Scope s e)
                               (\w -> scoped w <+> w)
                               (\r -> r)
instanceHolderScope =
  { held = \c written body -> heldScope (\sc -> body (s := Unit) sc) }

-- The runtime's custodians, for a world without them
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- (what `rooted` handles a computation into: one given the custodian
-- current, `Nothing` the one the runtime has where it runs)
type Rooting (e : HFunctor) a = Maybe Custodian.Custodian -> Co e a

rootingFunctor : (e : HFunctor) -> RawFunctor
rootingFunctor e =
  { f = \a -> Rooting e a
  , fmap = \g m -> \c -> bind (m c) (\x -> val (g x))
  }

-- (an operation of `e`, its computations given the custodian current;
-- a computation run with a custodian current, given it; and the
-- custodian's others, with the one current, the runtime's to answer)
rootingAlg : {e : HFunctor}
           -> {a : Type}
           -> {{dm : domain <: e}}
           -> (e <+> Custodian.custodian).f (\t -> Rooting e t) a
           -> Maybe Custodian.Custodian
           -> Co e a
rootingAlg (InL q) c =
  op (e.hmap (rootingFunctor e) (coFunctor e) (\m -> m c) q) val
rootingAlg (InR (Given (Under k s))) c =
  s (Co e a) (\b r -> case r of
    MkRunning body after -> bind (body (Just k)) (\v -> pure (after v)))
rootingAlg (InR q) c =
  op (dm (Holding (CustodyOp c (Custodian.custodianBy (\m -> m c) q)))) val

-- | `c`, its custodian's operations the runtime's custodians', as a
-- | world with the custodian's effect has them: the things its `with`s
-- | hold in their custody, a failure that reaches the root the
-- | program's.  So a world without that effect -- a `main` that does
-- | not name it -- has custodians all the same, through the domains',
-- | whose provider passes the operations on; and `c`'s is the only one
-- | in its world.  In a world with the custodian's effect, `c`'s are
-- | under the one current where it runs.
rooted : {e : HFunctor}
       -> {a : Type}
       -> {{dm : domain <: e}}
       -> Co (e <+> Custodian.custodian) a
       -> Co e a
rooted c =
  handle
    ({ M = \t -> Rooting e t
     , ret = \x -> \cur -> pure x
     , bind = \m f -> \cur -> bind (m cur) (\x -> f x cur)
     , malg = \p -> \cur -> rootingAlg p cur
     } :
       Handler (e <+> Custodian.custodian))
    c
    Nothing
